1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
OpsPilot - an AI operations agent for B2B billing support. This is a live dashboard over a real api + worker + Postgres stack; the model is a scripted replay, so no external AI service is called.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:16,000
The dashboard on a freshly brought-up demo stack: nothing parked, nothing failed, knowledge indexed.

3
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:24,500
A customer reports a duplicate charge on invoice INV-2026-384. The ticket form is pre-filled with the README's case.

4
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:31,500
Submitting creates the ticket and enqueues a run in the same transaction.

5
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:41,000
The run is received. The worker claims it on its next poll and starts the investigation - every step lands on the timeline as it happens.

6
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:54,000
The agent classified the ticket as a duplicate charge (0.93), retrieved the refund policy, and proposed a $129 refund on transaction TX-88219 - then PARKED. Nothing has been sent; the money waits for a human.

7
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,500
The Approvals inbox. The card shows exactly what will execute: the arguments, the risk level, the deterministic policy explanation - and the model's own justification, labelled untrusted.

8
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:08,500
The human approves. The click releases the run back to the worker - it does not execute the refund inside the request.

9
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:22,000
The worker executes the refund exactly once, idempotency-keyed, and the run completes with the full audit trail: 21 recorded steps, 5 cited sources.

10
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:25,400
Back on the Runs list: one completed run, fully traceable.

11
00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:33,400
Run it yourself: python scripts/demo_paths.py up. Paths B (reject) and C (model failure) ship with this video.
